This repository is licensed in TWO PARTS, split along what the code is for.
Every published package states its own license in its manifest, and every tree
below carries a copy of the license that governs it. This file only says which
is which.

## The seam is BUSL-1.1 - see LICENSE-BUSL

    c/magicseam
    go/magicseam
    ts/magicseam
    zig/magicseam

These IMPLEMENT the magic-seam wire protocol - the framing, the mTLS QUIC
transport, the connection handling. That is the distinction this list now draws,
and it is the one rust/seamwire failed: see below.

Change Date and Additional Use Grant are in LICENSE-BUSL; NOTICE summarises
them.

## The guest side and the vocabularies are Apache-2.0 - see LICENSE-APACHE

    c/periapsis
    go/gazer
    rust/perseid
    rust/seamwire
    ts/periapsis

Two kinds of thing, permissive for one reason.

Most are what a program you write links against to run ON Periapsis - the
periapsis:* WASI interfaces, the Perseid vocabulary, the aperture expression
language. A guest SDK that restricted its own consumers would defeat its purpose.

The rest are VOCABULARIES: they name things without implementing them. go/gazer
names a principal format; rust/seamwire names opcodes and capability tokens. A
vocabulary that restricts its speakers prevents the interoperation that writing
one down is for. Same reason, one level out.

***rust/seamwire MOVED HERE ON 2026-09-02, AND IT IS A RECLASSIFICATION RATHER
THAN A CARVE-OUT.*** It was in the BUSL list because it is the magic-seam
vocabulary and the seam is the product. But it IMPLEMENTS nothing: 461 lines, one
file, an EMPTY dependency table - measured with `cargo tree`, which reports a
single node - and no transport symbol anywhere in it. A crate that implemented an
mTLS QUIC transport could not have zero dependencies. The other four trees in the
BUSL list do implement; this one defines what the bytes mean.

A wire vocabulary that restricts its own speakers prevents exactly the
interoperation that publishing a protocol is for, which is the same principle
already stated below for the guest side.

THE ORDER OF THIS DECISION IS PART OF IT. The same reclassification was
CONSIDERED AND REFUSED four hours earlier, while periapsis-CE still needed the
answer to be yes - a definition following a convenience. It was made once nothing
turned on it: CE no longer depends on this crate at all. Same edit, opposite
motive, and only the second is a categorisation.

go/gazer joined them on 2026-09-02 and is the one that was not always here. It
is the Gazer identity vocabulary - a naming convention, its parser and its
builder, stdlib only - and it left go/magicseam because periapsis-CE is GPLv3
and depended on exactly those four symbols. BUSL's production-use conditions and
GPLv3 section 10 cannot both hold for a downstream recipient, so the half that
never touched the seam became permissive. go/magicseam keeps GazerPrincipal,
which needs the seam's own Caller type, and imports go/gazer: the dependency
runs BUSL -> Apache and must never run the other way.
